  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Friday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: A deep upper level trof continues to\nprogress thru Nevada this morning from west to east. However,\nupper level energy moving in from the upstream side of the trof\naxis will split the trof into two pieces by Saturday morning. An upper\nlevel closed low will form over southern California and Arizona, \nwhile the northern portion moves northeast and into Montana.\n\nShowers will be maintained across the forecast area thru most of\nthe day today though will dissipate from northwest to southeast as\nthe day progresses. Showers exit the forecast area completely by\nthe latter half of tonight. Thunderstorms are not anticipated\ntoday. Concurrently, cooler air aloft will filter in from the\nnorthwest. Snow levels, which had been near 9000 feet or higher \nfor the duration of the event, will lower to around 7000 feet. \nTiming may allow for snowfall, which had been restricted to the \nmountain summits, to reach some of the pass routes and foothills. \nAustin summit, Mountain City, and Jarbidge look most likely to \nreceive snowfall but any accumulation will be less than an inch \nwith the exception of Jarbidge where one to two inches are \nforecast. Winds will also continue to shift from southwesterly to\nnortherly today and this evening as the trof continues its \nprogression east and into Utah. Daytime high temperatures today\nwill be notably cooler as cooler air filters into Nevada from the\nnorthwest as noted. Look for highs in the mid 40s to mid 50s. \nOvernight lows will follow suite, and readings widespread in the \n20s are expected with the typical colder valleys residing in the \nmid to upper teens. \n\nWeather will be fair but cool this weekend under a modest warming\ntrend thru Monday. Daytime high temperatures Saturday will reside\nin the 50s though overnight lows remain cold under mostly clear\nskies and light to calm winds. Widespread readings in the 20s are\nagain expected with mid to upper teens in the typical colder\nvalleys. \n\nA modest low pressure system is still on track to move into the\nPacNW Sunday night. Most recent numerical model solutions bring\nthe track of this system slightly further south with the cold \nfront clipping northern Nevada from the northwest late in the day\nMonday/Monday night. Given the latest indicated trajectory of \nthis system, have opted to now include light rain and show showers\nin the forecast for northern Humboldt and Elko Counties. \nSignificant precipitation accumulation is not expected at this \ntime and rainfall/liquid equivalent snowfall accumulation will be \naround a tenth of an inch or less. Southwesterly breezes can also \nbe expected Monday afternoon. \n\nBeyond Monday, and thru the remainder of next week, weather looks\nto be fair with a modest upper level ridge positioned over the \nintermountain west. Temperatures will be mostly seasonal. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: There is high confidence for the\ncontinuation of showers thru today, while dissipating from\nnorthwest to southeast, and coming to an end tonight.\n\nThere is moderate confidence in the probability of light\nprecipitation across northern Humboldt and Elko Counties Monday.\nwith high confidence of fair weather thru the remainder of next \nweek. \n\nA blend of various model outputs utilized for PoP and weather\nparameters.",
